+          <span class="monospaced">
+           allow_ec_optimizations
+          </span>
+          is a one-way switch. Once enabled on a
+pool, the Ceph monitor refuses to clear the flag again, so rolling back
+requires draining and recreating the pool. Enable it only after validating
+that FastEC works for your workload on a test pool.
+         </td>
+        </tr>
+       </table>
+      </div>
+      <div class="sect5">
+       <h6 id="_enabling_fastec_on_a_pool">
+        Enabling FastEC on a pool
+        <a class="headerlink" href="#_enabling_fastec_on_a_pool" title="Permalink to this heading">
+        </a>
+       </h6>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         FastEC is enabled per pool via the
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          ceph
+         </span>
+         CLI. It can be set on both new
+and existing (populated) pools. No data migration or re-encoding takes
+place; only pool metadata changes.
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         To enable FastEC on a pool, run the following command against the data pool
+(typically named
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          &lt;pool-name&gt;-data
+         </span>
+         ):
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="listingblock">
+        <div class="content">
+         <!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight
+by Lorenzo Bettini
+http://www.lorenzobettini.it
+http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
+         <pre><tt>ceph osd pool <span style="font-weight: bold"><span style="color: #0000FF">set</span></span> <span style="color: #990000">&lt;</span>pool-name<span style="color: #990000">&gt;</span>-data allow_ec_optimizations <span style="color: #993399">1</span></tt></pre>
+        </div>
+       </div>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         Verify the result with:
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="listingblock">
+        <div class="content">
+         <!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight
+by Lorenzo Bettini
+http://www.lorenzobettini.it
+http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
+         <pre><tt>ceph osd pool get <span style="color: #990000">&lt;</span>pool-name<span style="color: #990000">&gt;</span>-data allow_ec_optimizations</tt></pre>
+        </div>
+       </div>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         The same command reports the current state for any EC pool, which is useful
+when auditing a cluster.
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         When the flag is set on a populated pool, be aware of the following:
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="ulist">
+        <ul>
+         <li>
+          <p>
+           <strong>
+            Primary re-election.
+           </strong>
+           FastEC marks data shards 1 through k-1 as
+  non-primary, so PGs whose primary was on one of those shards will
+  re-peer. This is a normal peering event and causes a brief I/O pause
+  per affected PG.
+          </p>
+         </li>
+         <li>
+          <p>
+           <strong>
+            Scrub interaction.
+           </strong>
+           Re-peering cancels any in-flight scrub on
+  affected PGs. Those PGs will need to be re-scrubbed after peering
+  completes.
+          </p>
+         </li>
+        </ul>
+       </div>
+      </div>
